---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import inventoryCode from '../../tools-code/inventory.py?raw';

// Draft how-to. Content is factual to the current inventory.py; refine in Finn's
// voice (write-like-finn) before this is final. Later phases (Gemini keywording)
// aren't documented yet — this covers Phase 1, the read-only inventory.
---
<BaseLayout title="fa-art-namer" description="A read-only way to inventory an art element library before a machine helps name it." register="art">
  <article>
    <h1>fa-art-namer</h1>
    <p><a href="/tools">← Tools</a></p>

    <p>An art practice built on collage accumulates thousands of cut-out elements —
      symbols, textures, scraps — and they arrive named like <code>IMG_4821.png</code>,
      <code>Untitled (2).png</code>, or a 90-character keyword-soup string. Before a
      machine can usefully help <em>name</em> and keyword that library, you need a
      truthful map of what's actually there. This is that map: a read-only inventory
      that never touches your source files.</p>

    <p>The division of labour is the point. The machine catalogues — dimensions,
      transparency, duplicates, suspicious names. The human keeps the judgment:
      which names are right, which chapters stay out of the shared vocabulary. The
      script only produces evidence; it renames nothing.</p>

    <h2>What it does</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Walks an art corpus and writes one row per image to <code>catalog.csv</code>.</li>
      <li>Reads width, height, and alpha via macOS <code>sips</code> — no Pillow install.</li>
      <li>Hashes each file (sha256) to catch duplicates.</li>
      <li>Flags <code>null-name</code> (device defaults), <code>soup-name</code> (over-long), and dirty whitespace.</li>
      <li>Marks style-divergent chapters as <code>quarantined</code> so they stay out of the core keyword framework.</li>
      <li>Leaves the schema columns (<code>new_name</code>, <code>subject</code>, …) blank, so the same CSV becomes the rename worklist for the next phase.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Run it</h2>
    <div class="code-block">
      <button class="copy" type="button">Copy</button>
      <pre><code># default root (canonical Drive 01_ART)
python3 inventory.py

# a local corpus, skipping the sha256 pass (faster on Drive)
python3 inventory.py --root ~/Desktop/01_ART --no-hash

# metadata only — no file hydration, safe first pass over Drive
python3 inventory.py --no-probe</code></pre>
    </div>

    <h2>The script</h2>
    <p>Standard library plus <code>sips</code>. Read-only by construction — it never
      renames, moves, or modifies a source file.</p>
    <div class="code-block">
      <button class="copy" type="button">Copy</button>
      <pre><code>{inventoryCode}</code></pre>
    </div>

    <p class="stub">Phase 1 of the pipeline. The Gemini-assisted keywording phase
      (a <code>GEMINI_API_KEY</code> in <code>config.env</code>) reads this catalogue
      and proposes names against the element-naming spec — documented next.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  document.querySelectorAll('.code-block').forEach((block) => {
    const btn = block.querySelector('.copy');
    const code = block.querySelector('code');
    if (!btn || !code) return;
    btn.addEventListener('click', async () => {
      try {
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText(code.innerText);
        btn.textContent = 'Copied';
        setTimeout(() => (btn.textContent = 'Copy'), 1500);
      } catch {
        btn.textContent = 'Press ⌘C';
      }
    });
  });
</script>
